# Reference lines: geom_abline

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_abline()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_abline.html), which covers
`geom_hline()` and `geom_vline()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows
ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code
and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

These geoms add reference lines to a plot: vertical ones at an `xintercept`, horizontal ones at a
`yintercept`, and diagonal ones given a `slope` and an `intercept`. Given as arguments, a line is
the same in every panel; mapped from a data frame, the lines can differ from panel to panel. Unlike
other geoms, they take nothing from the plot's mapping and do not change the plot's scales. Giggly
has none of them yet. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`,
which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write
`Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. It binds `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`, to `mtcars`, R's name for it.

## Vertical and horizontal lines

```r
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) + geom_point()
# Fixed values
p + geom_vline(xintercept = 5)
p + geom_vline(xintercept = 1:5)
p + geom_hline(yintercept = 20)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_vline()` and `geom_hline()`.

## Diagonal lines

```r
p + geom_abline() # Can't see it - outside the range of the data
p + geom_abline(intercept = 20)
```

With no arguments, the line is `y = x`, which runs below the panel, out of sight. Not in Giggly
yet: `geom_abline()`.

## A line of best fit

```r
# Calculate slope and intercept of line of best fit
coef(lm(mpg ~ wt, data = mtcars))
p + geom_abline(intercept = 37, slope = -5)
```

R fits `mpg` to `wt` with a linear model and prints its intercept and slope, which the line then
rounds. Not in Giggly yet: models fitted by `lm()`, and `geom_abline()`.

## Fitting the line with geom_smooth()

```r
# But this is easier to do with geom_smooth:
p + geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE)
```

`p` is the first example's plot, `mtcars`' weights against their miles per gallon, which Giggly
draws here. The smooth fits the same line as `lm(mpg ~ wt)`.

```elixir
p =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

p
|> Plot.geom_smooth(method: :lm, se: false)
|> render.()
```

## Lines that differ between facets

```r
# To show different lines in different facets, use aesthetics
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(~ cyl)
mean_wt <- data.frame(cyl = c(4, 6, 8), wt = c(2.28, 3.11, 4.00))
p + geom_hline(aes(yintercept = wt), mean_wt)
```

The facets are in Giggly: `facet_wrap(~ cyl)` is `Giggly.Plot.facet_wrap(plot, :cyl)`. Each line
comes from the row of `mean_wt` with its panel's `cyl`. Not in Giggly yet: `geom_hline()` and
mapping `yintercept`.

## Other aesthetics

```r
# You can also control other aesthetics
ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt, colour = wt)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_hline(aes(yintercept = wt, colour = wt), mean_wt) +
  facet_wrap(~ cyl)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_hline()` and mapping `yintercept`.